<h4 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Medication Admin Continuous</h4>

<div class="etl-guide-columns">
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 1: Create Medication Mappings</h5>
      <p class="text-sm mb-3">Before transforming your medication data, prepare the mapping files:</p>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm">
        <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg">
          <p><strong class="text-blue-300">a. Action Category Mapping:</strong> Map <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mar_action_name</code> → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mar_action_category</code>. The same CSV gives each category's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mar_action_group</code> (<code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">administered</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">not_administered</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">other</code>) — carry it into the table.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/Common-Longitudinal-ICU-data-Format/CLIF/blob/3.0/mCIDE/medication_admin_continuous/clif_medication_admin_continuous_action_categories.csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-300 hover:underline text-xs mt-1 inline-block">→ clif_medication_admin_continuous_action_categories.csv</a>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg">
          <p><strong class="text-blue-300">b. Med Category Mapping:</strong> Map <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code> → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code></p>
          <a href="https://github.com/Common-Longitudinal-ICU-data-Format/CLIF/blob/3.0/mCIDE/medication_admin_continuous/clif_medication_admin_continuous_med_categories.csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-300 hover:underline text-xs mt-1 inline-block">→ clif_medication_admin_continuous_med_categories.csv</a>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg">
          <p><strong class="text-blue-300">c. Route Category Mapping:</strong> Map <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_route_name</code> → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_route_category</code></p>
          <a href="https://github.com/Common-Longitudinal-ICU-data-Format/CLIF/blob/3.0/mCIDE/medication_admin_continuous/clif_medication_admin_continuous_med_route_categories.csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-300 hover:underline text-xs mt-1 inline-block">→ clif_medication_admin_continuous_med_route_categories.csv</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 2: Filter to Inpatient Hospitalizations</h5>
      <p class="text-sm">Filter your medication data to include only inpatient hospitalizations.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 3: Apply Mappings</h5>
      <p class="text-sm">Join the mapping files to add category columns.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 4: Decide Continuous vs Intermittent</h5>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm">
        <p class="mb-2"><strong>Keep by category:</strong> keep the rows whose <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code> maps to a <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">clif_medication_admin_continuous_med_categories.csv</code>. Then decide which table each row goes to, in this order:</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-decimal list-inside">
          <li><strong>Dose unit first.</strong> Look at the unit of the <strong>dose</strong> on the administration record, not the volume infusion rate.
            <ul class="space-y-1 mt-1 ml-4">
              <li>• <strong>Per-unit-time component</strong> (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mg/hr</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/min</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/kg/min</code>): the row is continuous and belongs here.</li>
              <li>• <strong>No time component</strong> (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mg</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">units</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">ml</code>): the row is intermittent and belongs in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">medication_admin_intermittent</code>.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><strong>MAR action second</strong>, when the dose unit is missing (NA) and cannot decide.
            <ul class="space-y-1 mt-1 ml-4">
              <li>• "given", "bolus", "push", "bolus from bag" and similar actions likely mean intermittent.</li>
              <li>• "rate change", "rate verify" and similar actions likely mean continuous.</li>
              <li>• Every site has its own MAR actions. Review the actions clearly associated with continuous drugs at your site and map them accordingly.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-2">This is how categories that appear in both CSVs (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">fentanyl</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">propofol</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">heparin</code>) are split.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-yellow-500/20 p-3 rounded-lg border border-yellow-400/30 text-sm mt-2">
        <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-2">IV fluids: a rate alone does not make a row continuous</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li>• An intermittent dose with a defined weight or volume can still have a rate attached. A volume-over-time rate such as mL/hr is not enough on its own.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Continuous</strong> means a rate with <strong>no defined</strong> volume or weight of drug to be given.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Intermittent:</strong> a dose of 2 L of lactated Ringer's given at a rate of 500 mL/hr. The dose is a defined volume.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Continuous:</strong> a lactated Ringer's order with a dose of 100 mL/hr and no defined volume to be given.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 5: Convert to the Category's med_dose_unit</h5>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm">
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li>• <strong>One unit per category:</strong> each <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> has one <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit</code> in the CSV (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">fentanyl</code> → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/hr</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">propofol</code> → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/kg/min</code>). Convert the raw rate into it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-yellow-500/20 p-3 rounded-lg border border-yellow-400/30 text-sm mt-2">
        <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-2">Accepted dose units:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li>• <strong>Listed unit:</strong> the mCIDE lists one <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit</code> for each <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>, for example <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/min</code> for <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">epinephrine</code> and <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mg/kg/hr</code> for <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">ketamine</code>.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Also accepted:</strong> the same unit with or without a per-kilogram weight basis: <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/kg/min</code> for <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">epinephrine</code> and <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mg/hr</code> for <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">ketamine</code>. Sites that record doses this way do not need to convert them.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Not accepted:</strong> a weight basis other than kilograms. For example, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">mcg/lb/min</code> must be converted.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 6 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 6: Record the Pump Rate</h5>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm">
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          <li>• <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">volume_infusion_rate</code> is the fluid volume per time (e.g. 2 mL/hr). It is separate from <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose</code>, which is the drug quantity per time.</li>
          <li>• Fill <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">volume_infusion_rate_unit</code> from the CSV's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">volume_infusion_rate_units</code> column: <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">ml/hr</code> for pumped infusions; <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">NA</code> for inhaled categories such as <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">albuterol</code> and <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">nitric_oxide</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- Step 7 - Inhaled Nitric Oxide -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 7: Special Case - Inhaled Nitric Oxide</h5>
      <div class="bg-yellow-500/20 p-3 rounded-lg border border-yellow-400/30 text-sm">
        <p>If inhaled nitric oxide is not in your medication admin table, refer to <strong>flowsheets data</strong>. The order typically goes in as an RT order (not medication order) and is documented in flowsheets rather than MAR. Map it to <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">nitric_oxide</code> with <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit = ppm</code>; it is a continuous-only category.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 8 - Combination Drugs -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 8: Handle Combination & Trial Drugs</h5>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">A. Combination Products</h5>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm mb-3">
        <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-2">Which med_category? Work through these in order:</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-xs list-decimal list-inside">
          <li><strong>The combination is its own <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>: one row, do not split.</strong> Search the CSV first. The 3.0 continuous CSV currently lists no combination drug as its own category, so for drugs this rule mostly applies to the intermittent table.</li>
          <li><strong>It is a combination fluid: one row.</strong> Many are listed explicitly (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">dextrose_5_sodium_chloride_0_45_pct</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">dextrose_5_lactated_ringers</code>). If yours is not, use the most appropriate <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">_other</code> category (e.g. <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">dextrose_other</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">sodium_chloride_other</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">balanced_crystalloid_other</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>The combination is not in the CSV: one row per active ingredient.</strong> Map each ingredient to its own <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>, with that ingredient's own dose. An ingredient that has no <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> in the CSV gets no row.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-yellow-500/20 p-3 rounded-lg border border-yellow-400/30 text-sm mb-3">
        <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-2">What goes in med_dose?</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs">
          <li>• <strong>Combination with its own category (rule 1):</strong> <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose</code> is the dose of the <strong>first drug named in the mCIDE <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code></strong>. The order of drugs in your raw <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code> does not matter.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Combination fluid (rule 2):</strong> <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose</code> is the volume per time, in the category's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit</code> (<code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">ml/hr</code>).</li>
          <li>• <strong>Combination split into ingredients (rule 3):</strong> each row carries its own ingredient's dose, in that category's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs">
        <p class="text-green-300 font-semibold mb-2">Example: "BUPIVACAINE 0.125%-FENTANYL 2 MCG/ML EPIDURAL INFUSION" running at 8 mL/hr</p>
        <p class="mb-2">There is no bupivacaine_fentanyl category, so it becomes two rows:</p>
        <div class="space-y-1 ml-2">
          <p>→ row 1: med_category = "bupivacaine", med_dose = 8, med_dose_unit = ml/hr</p>
          <p>→ row 2: med_category = "fentanyl", med_dose = 16, med_dose_unit = mcg/hr</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-gray-300">Both rows keep the full raw string in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code> and the same <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">admin_dttm</code>, so they can be traced back to one infusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs mt-2">
        <p class="text-blue-300 font-semibold mb-2">Unit Conversion (mL → the category's med_dose_unit):</p>
        <p>If the raw rate is in mL/hr, convert each row using the concentration of <em>that row's</em> ingredient in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code> (see Step 5). In the example, fentanyl at 2 mcg/mL running at 8 mL/hr is 16 mcg/hr.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-yellow-500/20 p-3 rounded-lg border border-yellow-400/30 text-sm mt-3">
        <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-2">Carrier fluids are not a second medication:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs">
          <li>• <strong>Map to the drug, not the fluid:</strong> a carrier fluid is not an ingredient to split out. When a drug is diluted in a carrier fluid such as normal saline, lactated Ringer's or Plasma-Lyte, the primary drug is the one the clinician is giving. Assign the row to that drug's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>.</li>
          <li>• <strong>Do not duplicate:</strong> do not add a second row for the carrier. e.g. "NOREPINEPHRINE 8 MG IN SODIUM CHLORIDE 0.9% 250 ML" → <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">norepinephrine</code> only, with no <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">sodium_chloride_0_9_pct</code> row.</li>
          <li>• <strong>When the fluid is the medication:</strong> use the fluid categories (<code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">sodium_chloride_0_9_pct</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">lactated_ringers_solution</code>, <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">plasma_lyte</code>) only when the fluid itself is what is given, such as a maintenance infusion.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs mt-2">
        <p><strong class="text-blue-300">Still not clear how to map a combination?</strong> This is a clinical judgement call. Have a clinician at your site decide the <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>, and apply the same decision to every row with that <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Trial Drugs & Placebos -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">B. Trial Drugs & Placebos</h5>
      <div class="bg-white/10 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm mb-3">
        <p class="mb-2">There is no <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">trial_drug</code> category. Map a trial drug to its active ingredient's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> like any other row; <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_group</code> then follows from the CSV.</p>
        <div class="bg-white/10 p-2 rounded text-xs mt-2">
          <p class="text-yellow-300 font-semibold mb-1">Examples:</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">"ACETAMINOPHEN (IRB 140122) 325 MG ORAL TAB"</p>
          <p class="mt-1">→ med_category: <span class="text-green-300">acetaminophen</span></p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-gray-300">"ACYCLOVIR OR PLACEBO CAPSULE (IRB 171591) 400 MG"</p>
          <p class="mt-1">→ the patient may have received placebo, so the active ingredient cannot be known: leave <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> unmapped (the raw string stays in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code>)</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-blue-300 mr-2">•</span>
          <span><strong>Brand Names:</strong> Map to the generic active ingredient's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code>; the raw brand string is preserved in <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_name</code></span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 9 -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Step 9: Final Cleanup</h5>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">•</span>
          <strong>Deduplicate:</strong> Remove duplicate rows
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">•</span>
          <strong>UTC Conversion:</strong> Convert all datetime fields to UTC timezone
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">•</span>
          <strong>Data Types:</strong> Ensure all variables match data dictionary specifications
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Data Quality Checks -->
    <div>
      <h5 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Data Quality Checks</h5>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">✓</span>
          Validate <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_category</code> mappings against mCIDE
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">✓</span>
          <span>Verify every row's <code class="bg-white/20 px-1 rounded">med_dose_unit</code> equals its category's unit in the CSV, or the same unit with or without a per-kilogram weight basis</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">✓</span>
          Check each combination is either one row in its own category (a combination drug or a combination fluid) or one row per active ingredient
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start">
          <span class="text-green-300 mr-2">✓</span>
          Check a drug diluted in a carrier fluid has no second row for the fluid
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
